    {
      "id": "ec73d350-f41b-4a7e-a7ee-74c98ce77a6d",
      "name": "Sticky Note - Overview",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -1920,
        -48
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": "#FBE704",
        "width": 640,
        "height": 880,
        "content": "## Send timesheet reminder emails to employees from a SharePoint CSV with Outlook\n\nReads a list of employees from a CSV file in SharePoint and sends each person a reminder email through Outlook, with their manager in CC. Afterwards the recipient columns in the CSV are cleared.\n\n## Who's it for\nControlling and team leads who need to remind employees to book their working hours.\n\n## How it works\n1. A schedule trigger starts the workflow every Monday and Wednesday at 1 p.m.\n2. The CSV file is downloaded from SharePoint and parsed.\n3. A Code node builds the recipient list (first name, email, manager email) and drops rows without a name or email.\n4. Outlook sends one reminder per employee, manager in CC.\n5. In parallel, the recipient fields are emptied and the CSV is uploaded back to SharePoint.\n\n## How to set up\n1. Connect your SharePoint and Outlook credentials.\n2. Select your site, folder, and CSV file in both SharePoint nodes.\n3. Match the column names in the Code node to your CSV.\n4. Adjust the email subject and text, then test with your own address first.\n\n## Requirements\n- Microsoft 365 account with SharePoint and Outlook\n- CSV file with name, email, and manager email columns\n- A process that refills the CSV before each run, otherwise no emails are sent\n\n## How to customize\nChange the schedule, the email text, or replace SharePoint and Outlook with Google Sheets and Gmail.\n\nTest with dummy data first. The workflow emails real people."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    }
